{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-211242",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{(1\\times 6)+5}{6}=\\frac{11}{6}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{3\\times 11}{1\\times 6}=\\frac{33}{6}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{33}{6}=\\frac{33÷3}{6÷3}=\\frac{11}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}=5\\,\\text{R}\\,1=5\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
